WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:02.640
Few sports test the limits of

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:04.847
professional athletes like cycling.

00:00:04.847 --> 00:00:06.257
[Cycling Commentator]
It's a furious threat.

00:00:06.257 --> 00:00:07.810
But it's not just human endurance

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:10.420
on the track that delivers
the winning formula,

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:12.450
it's human ingenuity off it.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:14.390
It's the world's fastest bike.

00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:15.510
In an elite sport,

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:17.767
the difference between success and failure

00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:20.323
is often the finest of margins.

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:28.270
This is base camp for one of

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:31.600
the most successful teams in global sport.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:33.890
Great Britain's track cyclists have topped

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:36.973
the medals tables at the
past three Olympic games.

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:40.443
And it's a team that keeps
churning out winners.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:45.900
We wanna be the fastest in the world,

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.820
we don't just wanna win the Olympics,

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:49.933
we wanna win in the fastest time ever.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:54.310
In a sport where races

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:57.670
are decided by as little
as 1/1000th of a second,

00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:00.523
Emily and her teammates are
obsessed with one thing:

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:04.083
marginal gains.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.130
A little margin of half a percent

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:10.370
will make that difference on the day.

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:11.877
[Cycling Commentator]
Turning the pressure on.

00:01:11.877 --> 00:01:13.310
And one of the best places

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:16.473
to find those tiny margins is on the bike.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:22.830
The team's key man for this
is an aerodynamics expert

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:25.940
and ex-Formula One motor racing engineer.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:29.030
My job is simply to use
technology and engineering

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:31.743
in any way I can to
make the team go faster.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:37.510
Cambridge University
Professor of Engineering,

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:39.820
Tony Purnell, designed the world-renowned

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:43.740
T5GB bike with manufacturer Cérvelo.

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:45.960
By dramatically reducing air resistance,

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:47.600
it helped the British team enjoy

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:50.290
its most successful Olympics ever.

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:52.220
It's the world's fastest bike.

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:55.867
The way those layers of
carbon fiber are constructed

00:01:55.867 --> 00:01:59.210
all makes for a lighter
and a stiffer bike,

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:01.293
without compromising the aerodynamics.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:04.360
All important milliseconds

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:06.073
were shaved off performance times

00:02:06.073 --> 00:02:09.117
by making the tiniest of design changes,

00:02:09.117 --> 00:02:10.823
even down to the chain.

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:16.640
When you cycle, a little
bit of the power you produce

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:19.500
gets lost in friction in the chain.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:23.230
If you can reduce that loss, it translates

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:27.570
into the athlete being that
little bit more powerful.

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:29.740
Using that chain would
have made the difference

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:32.313
in the games between the
silver and the gold medal.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:39.670
It's not just the bike

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:43.540
where aerodynamic perfection
is relentlessly pursued,

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:45.343
it's also the person on it.

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:49.450
The precise position of the rider

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:51.759
can make all the difference.

00:02:51.759 --> 00:02:52.957
[Cycling Commentator]
Always ahead of schedule,

00:02:52.957 --> 00:02:55.560
he was 45 seconds up after 40 kilometers.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.150
In 1996, Olympic Gold medalist

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:01.107
Chris Boardman broke the
one hour world record.

00:03:01.107 --> 00:03:01.940
[Cycling Commentator] As Boardman

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:04.380
settles into the superman
position, arms stretched

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:07.800
in front of his head, for
smoother aerodynamics.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:10.750
By pioneering his legendary
superman position.

00:03:12.933 --> 00:03:16.610
Today, this legacy lives
on at the state-of-the-art

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:19.213
Boardman Performance
Center, in Evesham, England.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:21.500
Bike design can absolutely help,

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.550
but we see that the
most significant portion

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:25.670
of the aerodynamic effects and the drag

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:27.370
is coming from the rider themself.

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:33.250
Sit down, tell me a little bit more about

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:35.290
the direction we want to take,
what do we want to look at.

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:36.780
Today, Jamie is helping

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:39.810
professional cyclist Dan Bigham decipher

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:43.950
his optimum body posture for
an upcoming team pursuit race.

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:46.082
In the wind tunnel, Dan is battling winds

00:03:46.082 --> 00:03:50.440
of over 60km/h to simulate
the drag conditions

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:52.440
he'll face on the track.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:55.290
His performance, and ultimately success,

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:57.930
could depend on a series
of almost imperceptible

00:03:57.930 --> 00:03:59.790
tweaks to his position on the bike.

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:01.570
Okay Dan, let's go for our first change,

00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:03.150
we'll do this on the fly.

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:04.600
Let's move the hands, please.

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:08.360
By moving his hands slightly forward,

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:11.680
and adjusting the gap between
them by just millimeters,

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:14.723
Dan speeds up by nearly
half a second per kilometer.

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:18.280
We're operating at a
world record place here.

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:19.710
So we've found some gains there,

00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:21.660
particularly from the hand open position,

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:23.310
which is absolutely worth having.

00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:27.340
Come race day, subtle changes like this

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:29.853
could add up to a big
advantage for Dan's team.

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:33.940
Me personally, I'm about 4/10ths
quicker just for my turn,

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:35.850
and if that gain was for
everybody in the team,

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:38.743
then we're one and a half to
two seconds quicker overall.

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:42.170
Cycling's reputation

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:43.763
has been damaged by doping.

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:48.300
But its pursuit of
legitimate marginal gains

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:50.943
still sets the pace for
many other disciplines.

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:54.730
Britain's world-beating cyclists

00:04:54.730 --> 00:04:57.047
face ever more intense
competition from rivals

00:04:57.047 --> 00:04:59.543
who are quickly learning how to innovate.

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:05.030
The pursuit of marginal gains

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:07.373
is about to get even more marginal.

